# The directory's enrollment store, exported still sealed, on a broker host. It reads the
# store with the broker's own etcd identity, so it needs no admin credential. systemd's
# StandardOutput=file: does not truncate, so a shorter export would keep the older one's tail
# and `port49 directory import` would refuse the file. The command's --out writes a new temporary
# file (O_EXCL, 0600, synced, removed if the export fails) in /var/backups/port49-broker (0700, the
# broker's, from tmpfiles.d) and the unit renames it to enrollments-<date>.json, so each day has its
# own copy and a failed run changes nothing. --out never replaces a file, so a temporary file a
# killed run left is removed first.
# It keeps fourteen days, and removes a temporary file a killed run left behind after a day. $$ and %% are systemd's escapes for $ and %.
# Unmeasured on a real host: run it once by hand (README) before relying on the timer.
# Restore with `port49 directory import`, which needs an admin and a key press.
[Unit]
Description=port49 enrollment export

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=port49-broker
Group=port49-broker
UMask=0077
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c 'set -eu; umask 077; d=/var/backups/port49-broker; f=$$d/enrollments-$$(date +%%F).json; rm -f "$$f.tmp"; if /usr/bin/port49-broker export-enrollments --out "$$f.tmp"; then mv "$$f.tmp" "$$f"; else rm -f "$$f.tmp"; exit 1; fi; /usr/bin/find "$$d" -name "enrollments-*.json" -mtime +14 -delete; /usr/bin/find "$$d" -name "enrollments-*.json.tmp" -mtime +1 -delete'
